Title: Po' Sandy
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 2
Production Code: 1x02

> FADE IN:

EXT. THE OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - DUSK

AMB: night insects, the creak of a loose shutter

A one-room pine house at the corner of the vineyard, unpainted, with a huge cracked brick chimney that a thin vine tries and fails to cover. The shutter has fallen from its hinge and lies in the jimson-weed.

Uncle Julius McAdoo stands in the doorway with his hat in his hand, looking into the lens, one hand resting on the doorframe as if feeling for a pulse.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Dey say wood doan feel de ax. Dey say a plank is des a plank. Now, I's seed w'at I's seed.

SFX: a long, thin groan from inside the house, like a board settling

He doesn't turn around.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Dat's de wind.

SFX: the groan again, lower, almost a word

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Mos'ly it's de wind.

MUSIC: STING - a single low saw-blade note, fading

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

INT. JOHN AND ANNIE'S HOUSE - DAY

A bright kitchen, built into the dwelling in the Northern fashion. Annie stands in the doorway in a morning dress, with a pencil, tapping a sketch. John reads over her shoulder.

JOHN
The house has a perfectly good kitchen.

ANNIE
It has a Northern kitchen. I want a Southern kitchen, in the back yard, apart from the house.

JOHN
I'll want to know why.

ANNIE
(a pause; a smile)
I'm afraid I don't know why.

John sighs. He has just learned the first law of marriage in a hot climate.

JOHN
Then I'll build it. And I'll save the cost of lumber by tearing down that old schoolhouse and using the planks. It's in a good state of preservation. I've made an estimate.

ANNIE
How many planks?

JOHN
Short several hundred feet. I'll buy those at the sawmill.

EXT. SANDY ROAD - DAY

The rockaway behind a gray mare. Julius drives, in his coachman's coat; John and Annie ride behind. The road runs down through forest, over a corduroy causeway through the swamp, and up a long hill.

SFX: wheels on rotted logs, the clop of the mare

EXT. THE SAWMILL - DAY

A clearing full of sawdust and sunlight. The foreman has gone off to a neighbor's farmhouse, probably to smoke and gossip. The Vineyard's rockaway waits, a few rods from the mill.

John, Annie, and Julius watch the hands lever a huge pine log into position on the carriage. The circular saw spins up with a high whir. The blade meets the log. The sound rises and falls like speech.

SFX: the saw, rhythmic, the cadence of a voice

Julius, on the box, goes still. His hands tighten on the reins. He shudders.

JULIUS
Ugh! But dat des do cuddle my blood!

ANNIE
What's the matter, Uncle Julius? Does the noise affect your nerves?

JULIUS
No, Mis' Annie. I ain' narvous. But dat saw, a-cuttin' en grindin' thoo dat stick er timber, en moanin', en groanin', en sweekin', kyars my 'memb'ance back ter ole times. 'Min's me er po' Sandy.

He draws out the "po' Sandy" until the words almost stop being words. Annie leans forward, hand at her throat.

ANNIE
Who was poor Sandy?

Julius takes off his hat. He looks at the saw as he begins.

JULIUS
Sandy wuz a nigger w'at useter b'long ter ole Mars Marrabo McSwayne...

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, sawing rhythm

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

A hot lane, white fence, a big house with wide porches. Marrabo McSwayne, easy-going, stands on the steps with a daughter and her new husband. Behind them, a good-natured man, strong, willing, carries a trunk.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Sandy wuz a monst'us good nigger, en could do so many things erbout a plantation, dat w'en Mars Marrabo's chilluns growed up en married off, dey all un 'em wanted dey daddy fer ter gin 'em Sandy fer a weddin' present.

Sandy sets down the trunk. The couple climb into a buggy. Sandy climbs on behind, with the trunk.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Mars Marrabo fix it by 'lowin' one er his chilluns ter take Sandy fer a mont' er so, en den ernudder fer a mont' er so, en so on dat erway.

Montage: Sandy, a month at a time, in a series of yards and kitchens; each with a different mistress at the door.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Tel bimeby it got so Sandy did n' hardly knowed whar he wuz gwine ter stay fum one week's een' ter de yuther.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - DAY

A speculator's wagon in the yard, a coffle of men and women sitting in the dust. Mars Marrabo shakes the trader's hand. In the line, a woman stands up as her name is called: Sandy's wife. A new woman is led out: Tenie, slight, level-eyed.

JULIUS (V.O.)
One time w'en Sandy wuz lent out ez yushal, a spekilater come erlong wid a lot er niggers, en Mars Marrabo swap' Sandy's wife off fer a noo 'oman.

Sandy returns on a mule, dusty, and sees an empty bed.

MARS MARRABO
(giving him a dollar)
I's monst'us sorry fer ter break up de fambly, Sandy, but de spekilater gun me big boot, en times is hard en money is skase.

Sandy looks at the dollar for a long moment.

INT. SANDY AND TENIE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Firelight. Tenie ladles something into a bowl. Sandy sits across from her. They are two people being careful with each other.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Sandy tuk on some 'bout losin' his wife, but he soon seed dey want no use cryin' ober spilt merlasses. En bein' ez he lacked de looks er de noo 'oman, he tuk up wid her atter she'd be'n on de plantation a mont' er so.

Montage, tender and short: Sandy sits up all night with Tenie when she is sick and goes to work in the morning as if he'd slept. Tenie darns his coat by the fire with her tongue in the corner of her mouth.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Sandy en Tenie had n' be'n libbin' tergedder fer mo' d'n two mont's befo' Mars Marrabo's ole uncle, w'at libbed down in Robeson County, sent up ter fin' out ef Mars Marrabo could n' len' 'im a good ban'.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - YARD - DAY

Mars Marrabo, mopping his brow, speaks to Sandy by the pump.

MARS MARRABO
Git ready ter go down ter Robeson tomorrer, Sandy. Fer a mont' er so.

INT. SANDY AND TENIE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Sandy at the fire, bitter and quiet.

SANDY
I'm gittin' monst'us ti'ed er dish yer gwine roun' so much. Here I is lent ter Mars Jeems dis mont', en I got ter do so-en-so; en ter Mars Archie de nex' mont', en I got ter do so-en-so; den I got ter go ter Miss Jinnie's: en hit's Sandy dis en Sandy dat, tel it 'pears ter me I ain' got no home, ner no marster, ner no mistiss, ner no nuffin. I can't eben keep a wife: my yuther ole 'oman wuz sol' away widout my gittin' a chance fer ter tell her good-by; en now I got ter go off en leab you, Tenie, en I dunno whe'r I'm eber gwine ter see you ag'in er no.

He looks into the fire.

SANDY (CONT'D)
I wisht I wuz a tree, er a stump, er a rock, er sump'n w'at could stay on de plantation fer a w'ile.

Tenie says nothing for a long time. Then:

TENIE
Sandy, is I eber tol' you I wuz a cunjuh 'oman?

SANDY
(blinking)
Naw.

TENIE
I ain' goophered nobody, ner done no cunjuh wuk, fer fifteen year er mo'. W'en I got religion I made up my mine I would n' wuk no mo' goopher. But dey is some things I doan b'lieve it's no sin fer ter do. Ef you doan wanter be sent roun' fum pillar ter pos', ef you doan wanter go down ter Robeson, I kin fix things so you won't haf ter.

SANDY
How?

TENIE
I kin turn you ter w'ateber you wanter be.

SANDY
Turn me inter what, Tenie?

She tries them on him, one at a time.

TENIE
Shill I turn you ter a rabbit?

SANDY
De dogs mought git atter me.

TENIE
Shill I turn you ter a wolf?

SANDY
Eve'ybody 's skeered er a wolf. I doan want nobody ter be skeered er me.

TENIE
A mawkin'-bird?

SANDY
A hawk mought ketch me. I wanter be turnt inter sump'n w'at'll stay in one place.

TENIE
I kin turn you ter a tree. You won't hab no mouf ner years, but I kin turn you back oncet in a w'ile, so you kin git sump'n ter eat, en hear w'at 's gwine on.

Sandy looks at his wife, a long time.

SANDY
Dat'll do.

EXT. THE SWAMP EDGE - NIGHT

AMB: frogs, a distant owl

Tenie leads Sandy by the hand to the edge of the swamp. She uncorks a bottle, sprinkles it over him, and walks backward away from him, speaking low.

SFX: wind rising out of nothing, a long creak like a mast under sail

A cloud crosses the moon. Where Sandy stood is a pine tree.

MUSIC: STING - a single saw-blade note

EXT. THE SWAMP EDGE - DAWN

Field hands on their way to work stop and stare at a tree they cannot remember. One shakes his head. Another says a sapling must have shot up overnight.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - YARD - MORNING

Dogs, barking, held on ropes. Marrabo and two white neighbors on horseback.

JULIUS (V.O.)
W'en Mars Marrabo 'skiver' dat Sandy wuz gone, he 'lowed Sandy had runned away.

The dogs, loosed, track straight to the base of the pine and stop. They bay at it, paw at it, try to climb it. They are led around the swamp to pick up a scent and break loose, back to the same tree.

MARS MARRABO
Sandy musta clim' up on de tree en jump' off on a mule er sump'n, en rid fur ernuff fer ter spile de scent.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Marrabo wanted ter 'cuse some er de yuther niggers er heppin' Sandy off, but dey all 'nied it ter de las'. En eve'ybody knowed Tenie sot too much sto' by Sandy fer ter he'p 'im run away whar she could n' nebber see 'im no mo'.

EXT. THE SWAMP EDGE - NIGHT

Tenie at the tree, in the dark, with the bottle. Sandy steps out of it, shaking. She holds him for a long time. They walk quickly, hand in hand, to the cabin.

INT. SANDY AND TENIE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Firelight, a table, a pot of food. They sit and eat and talk in low voices. Tenie watches the window. At the first gray, she walks him back to the tree.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey ha' ter be monst'us keerful, er e'se somebody would 'a' seed 'em. Tenie alluz turnt Sandy back in de mawnin' early, befo' anybody wuz a-stirrin'.

EXT. THE SWAMP EDGE - DAY

A woodpecker lands on the pine and begins to peck.

SFX: knock-knock-knock, hollow

INT. SANDY AND TENIE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Sandy, rolling up his sleeve. On his arm, a small round hole, as if a sharp stick had been driven in.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De nex' time Sandy wuz turnt back he had a little roun' hole in his arm.

Tenie sets a sparrow-hawk to watch the tree. The woodpecker, next morning, is snatched from the bark.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Nudder time, Mars Marrabo sent a nigger out in de woods fer ter chop tuppentime boxes.

A man with a hatchet cuts a deep box into the pine's side. Sap runs. In the cabin, Sandy has a raw scar down his leg.

TENIE
(working a poultice)
Hol' still.

JULIUS (V.O.)
It tuk Tenie nigh 'bout all night fer ter fix a mixtry ter kyo it up.

She sets a hornet to watch the tree. When the man comes back for a second box, the hornet stings so hard that the hatchet slips and nearly takes off his foot.

INT. SANDY AND TENIE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Tenie, with her hands on Sandy's shoulders.

TENIE
I's gwine ter turn you ter sump'n e'se. Me too. We be foxes, er sump'n. We run erway whar we kin be free en lib lack w'ite folks.

SANDY
When?

TENIE
Tomorrer night.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - YARD - DAY

The next day. A buggy at the front door. Young Dunkin McSwayne in a hurry, hat in hand, to his mother.

DUNKIN
My wife is monst'us sick, mammy. I wants you ter len' me a 'oman ter nuss her.

MRS. McSWAYNE
(without looking up)
Sen' Tenie. She's a good nuss.

Tenie, at the washtub, stops and straightens.

TENIE
Mistiss, lemme git my bonnet.

MRS. McSWAYNE
Yo' head-hank-cher's good ernuff.

TENIE
Lemme git my bes' frock.

MRS. McSWAYNE
You don't need no mo' frock. W'en dat one git dirty you kin git a clean one whar you gwine.

Tenie looks across the yard at the swamp, and at the pine. She climbs into the buggy.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Tenie had ter go 'long wid young Mars Dunkin ter his plantation, w'ich wuz mo' d'n twenty mile away, en dey wa'n't no chance er her seein' Sandy no mo' 'tel she come back home.

SFX: buggy wheels, going away

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - YARD - DAY

Mars Marrabo, on the porch, with a sketch of a new kitchen. He looks out at his lot of timber.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Wiles Tenie wuz away, Mars Marrabo tuk a notion fer ter buil' 'im a noo kitchen. En I dunno how it come to be so, but he happen fer ter hit on de ve'y tree w'at Sandy wuz turnt inter.

Two men at the foot of the pine. Their axes glance off; the blades barely mark the bark.

SFX: axes ringing and skidding on wood

The tree shakes, creaks, wobbles, and falls with a roar like a living thing.

MUSIC: STING - low strings, a long fall

EXT. THE MUD ROAD - DAY

A timber wagon, the log chained up. It sticks in the mud of the swamp; the mules strain. When it starts again the chains come loose, and the log rolls off down the hill into the trees.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Hit tuk nigh 'bout half a day mo' ter git it haul' up ter de sawmill.

EXT. THE SAWMILL - MORNING

Tenie, running up the long hill from the road, hair wild, a bottle in her hand. She is out of breath and crying.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Tenie come home de nex' mawnin'. W'en she seed de stump standin' dere, wid de sap runnin' out'n it, en de limbs layin' scattered roun', she nigh 'bout went out'n her min'.

At the mill, the hands have just rolled the big log onto the carriage. The saw is starting up.

TENIE
(running in, throwing herself on the log)
Sandy! Sandy, fergib me! I did n' mean ter! It ain' no fault er mine!

The mill hands stare at what they take for a crazy woman. Two seize her; one ties her arms with rope; they fasten her to a post and go back to their work.

SFX: the saw starts

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey started de saw up ag'in, en cut de log up inter bo'ds en scantlin's right befo' her eyes.

The saw bites. The log screams. The whole mill is filled with a moaning, groaning, shrieking sound that does not come from the blade.

Tenie tries to speak, to say the charm; her mouth is open and nothing comes. The saw goes on. The sawyer greases it; it makes no difference. The boards fall off the end in stacks.

MUSIC: STING - unresolved

INT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - SMOKEHOUSE - DAY

Tenie sits on the dirt floor with her knees up. Through the cracks, a bar of light. Outside, Mars Marrabo cursing.

MARS MARRABO (O.S.)
De spekilater w'at I got dat 'oman fum done fooled me, wukkin' a crazy 'oman off on me!

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - NEW KITCHEN - DAY

The new kitchen going up, planks nailed in place. A boy carries lumber on his shoulder. He stops. He puts his ear against a board. He steps back quickly.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - NEW KITCHEN - NIGHT

AMB: a long low moan, rising and falling with the wind

A woman at the kitchen door, with a lamp. She sees nothing. She hears something. She puts the lamp down and runs. Behind her, the kitchen door swings open and shut.

Tenie, in the dark, sits on the kitchen steps, leaning against the door-jamb. She murmurs low, as to a husband across a table, in no language anyone can make out.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey wa'n't naer nigger on de plantation w'at would n' rudder take forty dan ter go 'bout dat kitchen atter dark. Dat is, 'cep'n' Tenie.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - DAY

Mars Marrabo, hat pushed back, directing men with crowbars as they pull the kitchen down plank by plank.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Marrabo to' de kitchen down, en use' de lumber fer ter buil' dat ole school'ouse w'at you er talkin' 'bout pullin' down.

EXT. THE OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

New, pale, neat, with a child's slate by the door. A boy carries kindling.

EXT. THE OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - NIGHT

Tenie, old now, walks round the schoolhouse in the dark, running a hand along the boards. She talks to them.

INT. THE OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - MORNING

A winter morning. A boy opens the door to start the fire. On the floor, a woman, stiff and cold, one hand on a plank. No mark on her.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dere did n' 'pear ter be nuffin pertickler de matter wid her. She had des grieve' herse'f ter def fer her Sandy.

The boy kneels. He does not scream. He takes the shawl off his own shoulders and puts it over her.

EXT. McSWAYNE PLANTATION - PORCH - DAY

Mars Marrabo on the porch, indifferent, chewing a stem of grass.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Marrabo didn' shed no tears. Dey ain' much room in dis worl' fer crazy w'ite folks, let 'lone a crazy nigger.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Hit wa'n't long atter dat befo' Mars Marrabo sol' a piece er his track er lan' ter Mars Dugal' McAdoo,--my ole marster,--en dat 's how de ole school'ouse happen ter be on yo' place.

EXT. THE SAWMILL - DAY (PRESENT)

Julius, on the box, finishing, hat in his hand. The saw has stopped. The clearing is silent.

JULIUS
En folks sez dat de ole school'ouse, er any yuther house w'at got any er dat lumber in it, is gwine ter be ha'nted tel de las' piece er plank is rotted en crumble' inter dus'.

John, silent. Annie, staring, one hand over her mouth.

ANNIE
(low)
What a system it was, under which such things were possible!

JOHN
(disbelieving)
What things? Are you seriously considering the possibility of a man's being turned into a tree?

ANNIE
(quickly)
Oh, no. Not that.

A long pause. Then, softly, to no one:

ANNIE (CONT'D)
Poor Tenie.

Julius looks at his hands. John glances from his wife to the old man and back, and decides he has missed something. The foreman comes strolling back from his cigar.

FOREMAN
Morning, sir. What can I do for you?

JOHN
(after a beat)
I'd like to order some lumber.

INT. JOHN AND ANNIE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A big Northern bed. John is nearly asleep. Annie, whom he had thought asleep for half an hour, is suddenly sitting up.

ANNIE
John, I don't believe I want my new kitchen built out of the lumber in that old schoolhouse.

JOHN
(groaning)
You wouldn't for a moment allow yourself to be influenced by that absurdly impossible yarn Julius was spinning today?

ANNIE
I know the story is absurd, and I'm not so silly as to believe it. But I don't think I should ever be able to take any pleasure in that kitchen if it were built from that lumber. Besides, I think it would look better and last longer if the lumber were all new.

John stares at the ceiling.

JOHN
Of course you shall have your way.

He rolls over. She puts a hand on his back, and he gives in with good grace.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DAY

SUPER: "TWO WEEKS LATER."

John comes up the lane in his buggy, tired, with a bill from the sawmill in his hand. Annie meets him on the porch.

ANNIE
John, there has been a split in the Sandy Run Colored Baptist Church, on the temperance question. About half the members have come out from the main body and set up for themselves. Uncle Julius is one of the seceders, and he came to me yesterday and asked if they might not hold their meetings in the old schoolhouse.

JOHN
I hope you didn't let the old rascal have it.

ANNIE
I couldn't refuse him the use of the house for so good a purpose.

JOHN
(wearily)
And I'll venture to say you subscribed something toward the support of the new church.

Annie does not attempt to deny it.

JOHN (CONT'D)
What are they going to do about the ghost?

ANNIE
Uncle Julius says that ghosts never disturb religious worship. But if Sandy's spirit should happen to stray into meeting by mistake, no doubt the preaching would do it good.

John looks out across the vineyard at the old schoolhouse.

EXT. THE OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - EVENING

Lamps in the single window. A dozen people on benches inside, singing. Julius, in his Sunday coat, stands in the pulpit corner and leads.

SFX: a congregation singing, thin and strong, a hymn in a cracked chimney

On the other side of the singing, a groan, a board settling. The congregation, without losing the tune, begins to sing a little louder.

EXT. THE OLD SCHOOLHOUSE - NIGHT

Julius steps out into the dark, alone, after the others have gone. He looks into the lens, the lamp in his hand throwing light up on his face.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Ghosts is polite, ef you gives 'em somewhar ter set. Mos' ghosts is des folks w'at ain' got no place.

He looks at the schoolhouse.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I ain' sayin' it's so. I's sayin' de preachin' do 'em good.

He lifts the lamp and blows it out.

MUSIC: STING - a long saw-blade note, fading into singing

> FADE OUT.

THE END
